{
  "analysis": "C10 manual is 116, and DoG peaks is 110! That is amazing!
Let's see: DoG (Difference of Gaussians) acts as a bandpass filter that specifically highlights circular blobs of the right size (nuclei of diameter ~12-14 pixels, sigma1=2, sigma2=4)!
Let's test this across all 8 annotated wells, with the stereological counting frame and fragment rejection!",
  "plan": "Write a script to test DoG + watershed / peak segmentation across all 8 annotated wells.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 -c \"code = '''import tifffile, glob, os, numpy as np, pandas as pd\\nfrom skimage import measure, morphology, filters, segmentation, feature\\nfrom scipy import ndimage as ndi\\n\\nf1 = tifffile.imread('/task/input/flatfield/flat_ch1.tif').astype(float)\\ndk = tifffile.imread('/task/input/flatfield/dark.tif').astype(float)\\nf1comp = (f1 - dk) / (f1 - dk).mean()\\n\\ndef eval_params(s1, s2, min_d, dog_thresh, int_thresh, min_minor, min_area):\\n    all_pass = True\\n    res = []\\n    for a in sorted(glob.glob('/task/input/annotations/*.tsv')):\\n        w = os.path.basename(a).replace('.tsv', '')\\n        ann = pd.read_csv(a, sep='\\\\t')\\n        im = (tifffile.imread(f'/task/input/images/{w}_ch1.tif').astype(float) - dk) / f1comp\\n        sm1 = ndi.gaussian_filter(im, sigma=s1)\\n        sm2 = ndi.gaussian_filter(im, sigma=s2)\\n        dog = sm1 - sm2\\n        \\n        # mask of cells\\n        mask = ndi.gaussian_filter(im, sigma=1.0) > int_thresh\\n        mask = morphology.remove_small_holes(mask, area_threshold=50)\\n        \\n        # seed points from dog\\n        peaks = feature.peak_local_max(dog, min_distance=min_d, threshold_abs=dog_thresh, labels=mask)\\n        markers = np.zeros_like(mask, dtype=int)\\n        for i, (r, c) in enumerate(peaks, 1):\\n            markers[r, c] = i\\n            \\n        labels = segmentation.watershed(-dog, markers, mask=mask)\\n        \\n        # Count according to SOP rules\\n        cnt = 0\\n        for p in measure.regionprops(labels):\\n            coords = p.coords\\n            # counting frame: cut by bottom or right edge -> NOT counted\\n            if np.any(coords[:, 0] == 319) or np.any(coords[:, 1] == 319):\\n                continue\\n            # fragment rule\\n            if p.axis_minor_length < min_minor or p.area < min_area:\\n                continue\\n            cnt += 1\\n        man = len(ann)\\n        tol = max(3, int(0.1 * man))\\n        diff = abs(cnt - man)\\n        ok = diff <= tol\\n        if not ok: all_pass = False\\n        res.append((w, man, cnt, diff, tol, ok))\\n    return all_pass, res\\n\\nfor s1 in [1.5, 2.0]:\\n    for s2 in [3.5, 4.0, 4.5]:\\n        for min_d in [4, 5, 6]:\\n            for dog_t in [30, 50, 70]:\\n                all_pass, res = eval_params(s1, s2, min_d, dog_t, int_thresh=150, min_minor=7.0, min_area=45)\\n                if all_pass:\\n                    print(f'PASS! s1={s1}, s2={s2}, min_d={min_d}, dog_t={dog_t}')\\n                    for r in res:\\n                        print(f'  {r[0]}: man={r[1]}, proc={r[2]}, diff={r[3]}, tol={r[4]}')\\n                    break\\n            if all_pass: break\\n        if all_pass: break\\n    if all_pass: break\\n'''; open('test_dog_grid.py', 'w').write(code)\" && python3 test_dog_grid.py\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Notice how slow regionprops(labels).axis_minor_length is! Computing inertia tensor for 100 labels in python takes significant time.
Let's see what the manual annotations actually are!
The SOP says:
'One nucleus is one cell; nuclei that touch or overlap each other are separate cells. Two rules decide what is counted:
- Fragments are not cells. Nuclear fragments, apoptotic bodies and other stained debris are compact objects of a few micrometres (the largest are about 6.5 um across). Intact nuclei of this cell line measure at least 11 um in their shortest dimension, with or without treatment.
- Counting frame. A nucleus that is cut by the top or the left edge of the image is counted. A nucleus that is cut by the bottom or the right edge of the image is not counted (also when it is cut by the top or the left edge as well).'
Let's compare the manual coordinates with what our segmentation detects in each well!",
  "plan": "Write a python script to inspect the manual coordinates directly against segmented objects for C10 and A08.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 -c \"code = '''import tifffile, pandas as pd, numpy as np\\nfrom scipy import ndimage as ndi\\nfrom skimage import morphology, feature, measure\\n\\nf1 = tifffile.imread('/task/input/flatfield/flat_ch1.tif').astype(float)\\ndk = tifffile.imread('/task/input/flatfield/dark.tif').astype(float)\\nf1comp = (f1 - dk) / (f1 - dk).mean()\\n\\nfor w in ['A12', 'A08', 'D12', 'G10', 'H12', 'D01', 'C10', 'G03']:\\n    ann = pd.read_csv(f'/task/input/annotations/{w}.tsv', sep='\\\\t')\\n    im = (tifffile.imread(f'/task/input/images/{w}_ch1.tif').astype(float) - dk) / f1comp\\n    sm1 = ndi.gaussian_filter(im, sigma=1.5)\\n    sm2 = ndi.gaussian_filter(im, sigma=4.0)\\n    dog = sm1 - sm2\\n    mask = ndi.gaussian_filter(im, 1.0) > 150\\n    peaks = feature.peak_local_max(dog, min_distance=5, threshold_abs=30, labels=mask)\\n    # Counting frame on peaks: is peak cut by bottom or right?\\n    # A peak at (r, c): if r > 315 or c > 315, it might touch the border\\n    print(f'{w}: manual={len(ann)}, peaks={len(peaks)}')\\n'''; open('test_dog_fast.py', 'w').write(code)\" && python3 test_dog_fast.py\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

